WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.469
-Our next guest is
the co-creator and star

00:00:02.469 --> 00:00:04.203
of the Showtime series
"Work in Progress,"

00:00:04.203 --> 00:00:05.371
which airs Sunday nights.

00:00:05.371 --> 00:00:06.538
Let's take a look.

00:00:06.538 --> 00:00:09.008
-Don't you dare move.

00:00:09.008 --> 00:00:10.376
Trash can! Trash can!

00:00:10.376 --> 00:00:12.245
Oh [bleep]
[ Shouts indistinctly ]

00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:14.080
Oh, God, have I already [bleep]
this up?

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.448
You're so [bleep] sexy!

00:00:15.448 --> 00:00:16.816
I can't stand it!

00:00:19.051 --> 00:00:21.887 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.887 --> 00:00:23.856
-Where were we?

00:00:23.856 --> 00:00:25.391
-Take me there, Bobby.

00:00:25.391 --> 00:00:29.162 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:33.466
[ Laughter ]

00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:34.600
-Please welcome back --

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.569
Please welcome to the show
Abby McEnany.

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:37.871
How are you, Abby?

00:00:37.871 --> 00:00:39.505
-Hey, Seth. How are you?
-This is -- I'm great.

00:00:39.505 --> 00:00:41.608
-I still can't believe that --

00:00:41.608 --> 00:00:43.343
-So, this is really something
here.

00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:45.945
This is -- Obviously that was
Vincent D'Onofrio.

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:47.514
-I know!

00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:49.215
-Your character is a fan of him,

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:51.784
but this is the real, true thing
about you your whole life.

00:00:51.784 --> 00:00:55.288
-I don't know my whole life.
I'm very old.

00:00:55.288 --> 00:00:57.857
A very long time. Yeah.

00:00:57.857 --> 00:00:59.992
Oh, dude, yeah.
I'm very much a fan.

00:00:59.992 --> 00:01:03.229
In fact,
for my birthday in 2010,

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:04.998
one of my friends made me this.

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:07.500
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.470
-That's my face over
Detective Eames' head.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.373
Listen, yeah,
I can't even stand it.

00:01:13.373 --> 00:01:15.274
I can't believe it.
It's crazy.

00:01:15.274 --> 00:01:18.210
-So, you -- how did it happen
that you reached out

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:21.113
to Vincent D'Onofrio
and he agreed to do the show?

00:01:21.113 --> 00:01:23.149
-Honestly, if you
ever talked to him,

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:24.650
I don't know why he agreed.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:26.385
I don't --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:26.385 --> 00:01:28.154
Also, I know this is so --

00:01:28.154 --> 00:01:30.155
I'm so -- I'm so honored
to meet you.

00:01:30.155 --> 00:01:32.559
Okay, I'm very nervous,
but this is really an honor.

00:01:32.559 --> 00:01:34.059
So thank you for having me.

00:01:34.059 --> 00:01:37.096
-I'm so happy to meet you too,
and we both came up in Chicago,

00:01:37.096 --> 00:01:38.664
and we know a lot of
the same people,

00:01:38.664 --> 00:01:40.733
all of whom speak very highly
of you.

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:43.736
-Okay, well, same here.

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:46.272
So maybe we need to do
some digging.

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:48.808
But anyways, like, really,
honor, it's been an honor --

00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:50.844
it's been an honor.
You're still on.

00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:54.480
Okay. Anyways, so, my --
you know, my --

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.548
my partner, Lilly Wachowski,

00:01:56.548 --> 00:01:59.818
and she had met Vincent
a long, long time ago.

00:01:59.818 --> 00:02:03.223
So, wrote a really lovely e-mail
to him and just kind of

00:02:03.223 --> 00:02:05.825
explained the show and what we
were trying do this season.

00:02:05.825 --> 00:02:08.661
And we reached out
actually in May of 2020.

00:02:08.661 --> 00:02:10.596
And, you know, we -- we were --

00:02:10.596 --> 00:02:12.831
we had like four different
start dates for season two.

00:02:12.831 --> 00:02:15.902
You know, and so, once
we, like, knew our date

00:02:15.902 --> 00:02:18.103
and we were like,
"Hey, we only need a day.

00:02:18.103 --> 00:02:19.105
We'll fly to New York.

00:02:19.105 --> 00:02:21.107
We'll do a green-screen day."

00:02:21.107 --> 00:02:23.075
And he ended up flying here.

00:02:23.075 --> 00:02:26.445
And I still, like, "Whoa."

00:02:26.445 --> 00:02:28.114
-That's really cool.

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:30.850
Now, don't take this
the wrong way.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:33.920
I have my doubts you were able
to keep your cool around him.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:37.157
[ Laughter ]

00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:39.425
How'd you do
when you first met him?

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:41.627
-I have to say, I thought
you were better at,

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:44.063
like, really
understanding folks.

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:46.833
No, absolutely.
It was -- It was outrageous.

00:02:46.833 --> 00:02:50.970
We met him on -- We met him
on Zoom like the week before.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:55.308
Lilly and I, like, and "Vincent"
Zoomed just to check in,

00:02:55.308 --> 00:02:57.877
'cause, you know, here's, like,
this big star coming to Chicago

00:02:57.877 --> 00:02:59.679
to do this thing for us,

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:02.281
and, you know, just finding out,
like, how he works and, like,

00:03:02.281 --> 00:03:04.183
what could we do
to make the day better

00:03:04.183 --> 00:03:08.488
and explain to him
what it was and stuff.

00:03:08.488 --> 00:03:11.557
We actually have another really
fun guest star this year.

00:03:11.557 --> 00:03:16.128
It's Audie Cornish from NPR,
who's just a -- holy smokes.

00:03:16.128 --> 00:03:19.598
So, we met her on Zoom
in January.

00:03:19.598 --> 00:03:23.502
So Lilly and then Blythe Haaga,
who is a producer

00:03:23.502 --> 00:03:28.007
and one of our directors, and I
were talking to Audie on Zoom.

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:32.177
And I was kind of bouncing on
the couch, I was so nervous.

00:03:32.177 --> 00:03:35.514
And then, when we were about
to meet Vincent,

00:03:35.514 --> 00:03:36.782
I just told Lilly like,

00:03:36.782 --> 00:03:38.250
"Hey, when it starts
getting like that,

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:42.588
just text me in all caps saying,
'Audie, calm down. Calm down.'"

00:03:42.588 --> 00:03:44.790
And then, like, of course,
'cause I can't keep anything

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:47.660
to myself, I told Vincent that
about 15 minutes in.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.428
-[ Laughs ]
-"Just so you know, I promise --

00:03:49.428 --> 00:03:52.965
I asked Lilly to promise me
to tell me if I got too creepy."

00:03:52.965 --> 00:03:55.601
-Well, obviously you didn't,
because he flew out to Chicago.

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:56.936
But I want to talk about
something

00:03:56.936 --> 00:03:58.704
that's incredibly impressive.

00:03:58.704 --> 00:04:01.473
Obviously, anytime someone
creates a show for themselves

00:04:01.473 --> 00:04:04.911
that they write and star in,
that's a big deal.

00:04:04.911 --> 00:04:06.679
But you had kind of never --

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:08.982
you sort of skipped
all the other steps.

00:04:08.982 --> 00:04:11.851
This was, like, the first set
you had ever been on, right?

00:04:11.851 --> 00:04:15.521
-Right. I mean, I would say --
like, skipping steps, I mean,

00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:17.089
I've been doing this [bleep]
for a long time.

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:19.058
-Sure, yes. Understood.

00:04:19.058 --> 00:04:21.027
-But, no. You know what I mean.

00:04:21.027 --> 00:04:23.562
But, yeah, like, it's wild.
It's wild.

00:04:23.562 --> 00:04:25.764
Yeah, I had auditioned
every once in a while.

00:04:25.764 --> 00:04:26.932
Got to say, nobody wants

00:04:26.932 --> 00:04:29.969
a big, old, fat, woman queer
to sell burgers.

00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:31.804
You know what I'm talking about?

00:04:31.804 --> 00:04:34.306
But I'm a really horrible
auditioner, too. Anyways.

00:04:34.306 --> 00:04:36.509
So, yeah, it was -- it's wild.

00:04:40.245 --> 00:04:42.648
The learning curve was bonkers.

00:04:42.648 --> 00:04:44.283
Like, I still can't believe it.

00:04:44.283 --> 00:04:46.152
And, you know, having
Lilly Wachowski,

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:50.222
who, you know, changed cinema
as your partner,

00:04:50.222 --> 00:04:51.590
who really knows
how things work,

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:53.592
is, like, totally supportive.

00:04:53.592 --> 00:04:54.860
It has been amazing.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:57.496
Yeah, I learned stuff every day.
Every day.

00:04:57.496 --> 00:05:00.232
And all these terms.

00:05:00.232 --> 00:05:01.901
I was like,
"Is that an industry term?"

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:03.602
They're like,
"No, we're going to lunch."

00:05:03.602 --> 00:05:04.803
I'm like, "Okay. Alright."

00:05:04.803 --> 00:05:06.906
I don't know...

00:05:06.906 --> 00:05:10.943
But everybody's -- like, really,
our crew's so wonderful.

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:13.979
-Chicago must be very happy that
you're doing the show there,

00:05:13.979 --> 00:05:17.149
especially as you sort of
got your comedy start

00:05:17.149 --> 00:05:19.351
there doing Second City.
I know you did Tour Co.

00:05:19.351 --> 00:05:23.889
I'm always fascinated, delighted
to hear people's worst

00:05:23.889 --> 00:05:27.393
Tour Co shows when they were
going around the country,

00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:28.894
doing improv for people.

00:05:28.894 --> 00:05:30.562
Do you have any particularly
bad ones?

00:05:30.562 --> 00:05:33.432
-Well, yeah. We were in
University of South Carolina

00:05:33.432 --> 00:05:36.536
once, and it was like the week
before school started.

00:05:36.536 --> 00:05:39.271
So they have all these free
events for incoming freshmen.

00:05:39.271 --> 00:05:42.808
And so, they booked Second City,
and we were playing --

00:05:42.808 --> 00:05:45.043
it basically looked like one of
those hotel ballrooms

00:05:45.043 --> 00:05:46.679
with the accordion doors.

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:48.781
It was just crap, right?

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:50.916
There were, like, I don't know,
rows and rows of chairs

00:05:50.916 --> 00:05:52.284
that nobody was in.

00:05:52.284 --> 00:05:56.356
And then our stage manager
couldn't really hear us.

00:05:56.356 --> 00:05:58.458
It was like a crappy
pipe and drape.

00:05:58.458 --> 00:06:02.762
And then the Fighting Gamecock
is their mascot.

00:06:02.762 --> 00:06:05.965
So, the announcer was like,

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:09.001
"Okay, next up,
the Second Nation."

00:06:09.001 --> 00:06:12.672
And then the Fighting Gamecock
is on stage, like, dancing,

00:06:12.672 --> 00:06:15.575
and our, like, stage manager's
yelling, "It's Second City!"

00:06:15.575 --> 00:06:18.611
And then the guy goes,
"Also, free Dippin' Dots."

00:06:18.611 --> 00:06:22.148
So, then, like in the same room,
like, the 10 people that like,

00:06:22.148 --> 00:06:23.782
"Free Dippin' Dots?"
So, at the end,

00:06:23.782 --> 00:06:25.485
like at the back of this room,

00:06:25.485 --> 00:06:28.854
there were about 150 people
in line for free Dippin' Dots.

00:06:28.854 --> 00:06:31.424
Yeah, it wasn't
exactly seamless.

00:06:31.424 --> 00:06:33.826
[ Laughter ]

00:06:33.826 --> 00:06:35.694
-You have another cool
guest star,

00:06:35.694 --> 00:06:38.497
which was Julia Sweeney,
who, of course, was

00:06:38.497 --> 00:06:42.167
a celebrated "SNL" cast member
over the years.

00:06:42.167 --> 00:06:44.704
She wrote and created
the character Pat,

00:06:44.704 --> 00:06:49.007
who became, you know,
a character that was, you know,

00:06:49.007 --> 00:06:51.144
criticized as the years passed.

00:06:51.144 --> 00:06:53.713
And you reached out to her,
and she was willing to sort of

00:06:53.713 --> 00:06:55.414
play ball and be
a part of your show.

00:06:55.414 --> 00:06:58.684
-Absolutely. She's been, like,
so generous and open

00:06:58.684 --> 00:07:02.187
about this from the very,
very get-go.

00:07:02.187 --> 00:07:06.592
She was in our pilot
that we shot here in Chicago.

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:09.828
And we just raised money,
and she got paid 125 bucks.

00:07:09.828 --> 00:07:12.164
You know, the SAG scale.

00:07:12.164 --> 00:07:14.200
I mean, just outrageously kind.

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:17.736
And yeah, and we -- you know,
she's never asked us to, like,

00:07:17.736 --> 00:07:20.739
change, like, "Oh, please don't
say this about my character,

00:07:20.739 --> 00:07:22.842
or please" --
She's just been totally willing

00:07:22.842 --> 00:07:25.478
to have those conversations, and
we have them off screen, too.

00:07:25.478 --> 00:07:28.480
I mean, you know, we're just
not totally in the same space.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:30.716
And I have to say,
when Pat came out, I loved Pat.

00:07:30.716 --> 00:07:32.618
And I think really, it's --

00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:35.020
and I understand, like,
it's problematic.

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:39.025
But I think it's also, anytime,
like, people are like, "Oh, wow,

00:07:39.025 --> 00:07:41.527
there's somebody who I don't
like walking down the street.

00:07:41.527 --> 00:07:44.029
Let me use this thing
as a bigoted." You know?

00:07:44.029 --> 00:07:46.299
Which is always a delight.

00:07:46.299 --> 00:07:48.133
You know, Seth? I mean, come on.

00:07:48.133 --> 00:07:50.503
It's hard enough to leave
the house, my man.

00:07:50.503 --> 00:07:52.471
Come on, dawg. Anyways.

00:07:52.471 --> 00:07:56.609
Nobody's yelling it out as like,
"Hey, hi, Pat!"

00:07:56.609 --> 00:07:57.810
You know, it's just like --

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:00.313
It's not filled with
a lot of hubbub-hubbub.

00:08:00.313 --> 00:08:01.714
You know what I'm talking about.

00:08:01.714 --> 00:08:05.117
-Hey, first of all, I'm so happy
that you were here.

00:08:05.117 --> 00:08:06.953
Congratulations on the show.

00:08:06.953 --> 00:08:09.121
It's such an accomplishment.

00:08:09.121 --> 00:08:12.224
And, hopefully next time you can
be here with us in person.

00:08:12.224 --> 00:08:13.725
-That's an honor.

00:08:13.725 --> 00:08:16.228
And also I really feel this is
going to go into "Corrections."

00:08:16.228 --> 00:08:18.964
"Corrections -- we never should
have had McEnany on."

00:08:18.964 --> 00:08:20.232
[ Laughter ]
So, I don't know.

00:08:20.232 --> 00:08:23.001
I'll check it out. I'll check it
out on Friday, okay?

00:08:23.001 --> 00:08:25.104
-Well, anything to draw eyes to
"Corrections."

00:08:25.104 --> 00:08:26.338
You know, anything.

00:08:26.338 --> 00:08:28.006
If we can just get you
to promise to watch it,

00:08:28.006 --> 00:08:29.274
that would mean --
-I've got four eyes here, sweet.

00:08:29.274 --> 00:08:31.476
So I got it.
Seth, seriously, it's an honor.

00:08:31.476 --> 00:08:32.978
Thank you so much.
-Thank you.

00:08:32.978 --> 00:08:34.546
The second season of
"Work in Progress"

00:08:34.546 --> 00:08:35.548
airs Sunday on Showtime.

